## Solution to Grade

---
title: "Offline-First Application Sync"
category: "Strategic Problems"
difficulty: "Hard"
tags: ["offline-first", "sync", "conflict-resolution", "mobile", "crdt", "replication"]
---

## Overview

Field-operations apps (inspections, deliveries, maintenance, incident response) must remain fully usable for days without reliable connectivity. The core requirement is a local-first UX with correct reconciliation when devices reconnect, including concurrent edits, retries, out-of-order delivery, partial uploads, and schema evolution.

This design syncs by **replicating an operation log**. Clients record immutable operations locally and upload them in batches. The server durably stores operations, deterministically merges them into current entity state, and returns an opaque server-issued checkpoint that clients use to pull deltas efficiently.

### Goals
- Local-first UX: offline CRUD with fast local reads/writes.
- Resumable, bandwidth-efficient two-way sync with long partitions and reconnect storms.
- Idempotent ingestion and replayable history for audit/debug/rebuild.
- Deterministic convergence with clear conflict handling (auto-merge when safe, review when required).

### Non-Goals
- Real-time collaborative editing (e.g., shared cursors) for rich text.
- Perfectly preventing offline access after membership revocation (addressed as an optional hardening path).

---

## Requirements

### Functional Requirements
- Offline CRUD for core entities (`WorkOrder`, `Asset`, `Inspection`, `FormResponse`) with durable local persistence.
- Two-way sync: push local ops; pull remote ops since last checkpoint.
- Deterministic conflict handling: auto-merge when safe; flag conflicts for review.
- Attachment sync (photos/videos/signatures) with resumable uploads and deduplication.
- Multi-device per user with eventual convergence.
- Idempotent retries: duplicated ops/requests must not corrupt state.
- Server-enforced access control (tenant/project/site/user scopes) and filtered deltas.
- Auditability: reconstruct who changed what, when, and from which device/app version.

### Non-Functional Requirements (Concrete Targets)

#### Scale (Illustrative but Realistic)
Assume 50K DAU field workers and 5M MAU viewers/admins.

- **Operations generated**:
  - Typical: ~5M ops/day
  - Heavy: ~10M ops/day
- **Reconnect storms**:
  - 10K devices / 10 minutes; average 1K ops backlog → ~16.7K ops/s ingestion
- **Worst-case backlog**:
  - 50M ops over hours; handled via throttling, batching, and progressive catch-up
- **Attachments**:
  - 2–10 GB/device/month; peak upload goes direct to object storage

#### Latency & UX
- **Handshake** P50 50ms / P99 250ms (auth + metadata).
- **Push batch** P50 200ms / P99 1,200ms.
- **Pull batch** P50 150ms / P99 900ms.
- **Time-to-catch-up**: <2 minutes for 10K ops on good LTE via batching + compression + paging.
- **Offline reads/writes** on device: <20ms P50.

#### Availability & Durability
- **Sync APIs**: 99.95% monthly availability (regional).
- **Auth/token validation**: 99.99%.
- **Durability**: RPO ≈ 0 for acknowledged operations.
- **RTO**: 1 hour for regional failover (configurable).

#### Consistency Model
- Eventual consistency for business entities across devices with deterministic convergence.
- Strong consistency for authentication, device registration, and checkpoint issuance.
- Per-entity causal merges using per-actor sequencing and deterministic merge rules.

### Constraints & Assumptions
- Mobile clients use SQLite and maintain a durable local op log.
- Client wall clocks are untrusted.
- Compliance: PII in forms; encryption at rest/in transit; audit retained 1–7 years (configurable).

---

## Simplified Architecture

```mermaid
graph TB
  C["Mobile App"] -->|HTTPS| E["Edge"]
  E --> A["OIDC Auth"]
  E --> S["Sync API"]
  S --> P["Postgres"]
  C -->|Pre-signed URL| O["Object Storage"]
  S --> O
```

### Key Ideas
- **One server component** (`Sync API`) handles handshake, push, pull, merges, conflicts, and attachment coordination.
- **One primary database** (Postgres) stores the operation log, materialized entity state, checkpoints, conflicts, and attachment metadata.
- **Server-issued checkpoints** provide efficient “pull since last known point” without relying on client clocks.
- **Attachments bypass the Sync API** using pre-signed uploads; the Sync API validates and commits metadata.

---

## Components

## Mobile Client (Offline Engine)

**Responsibilities**
- Local-first reads/writes on materialized entity tables.
- Durable local append-only op log for retries, batching, and compaction.
- Background sync with exponential backoff + jitter and OS-friendly scheduling.
- Conflict UI for “needs review” items while preserving user intent.

**Key Decisions**
- Stable IDs generated client-side (`UUIDv7` or `ULID`) so references work offline.
- Local op log separate from entity tables to support retries and compaction.
- Server deltas are applied idempotently to local state.

**Client Compaction**
- After server acknowledges a contiguous `device_seq` range, client purges those ops.
- Optional local snapshot + tail ops to speed cold start on large datasets.

---

## Edge + Auth

**Edge Responsibilities**
- TLS termination, basic rate limiting, request size limits, and routing.
- Optional compression negotiation (zstd/gzip).

**Auth Responsibilities**
- OIDC/OAuth2 with short-lived access tokens and refresh tokens.
- Tokens include `tenant_id`, `user_id`, and coarse scopes for authorization decisions.

---

## Sync API (Single Service)

**Responsibilities**
- Handshake/capability negotiation.
- Push ingestion: validate, authorize, persist ops, apply deterministic merges, issue checkpoints.
- Pull: generate deltas since checkpoint with server-side authZ filtering.
- Conflict classification + “needs review” workflow records.
- Attachment init/commit (pre-signed URLs; metadata commit).

**Ingestion Semantics**
- Clients deliver at-least-once; server guarantees exactly-once effects using idempotency keys:
  - Unique `(tenant_id, device_id, device_seq)` per operation
  - Optional request `Idempotency-Key` for whole-batch retries
- Acknowledgment occurs only after ops are durable and their merges are applied.

**Conflict Handling**
- Field-level merges for safe types.
- Semantic rules for workflow invariants (state machines, approvals) that produce explicit conflicts.

---

## Storage (Postgres-Only)

Postgres is the single source of truth for:
- Operation history (append-only).
- Materialized entity state for fast reads.
- Checkpoints per device/session.
- Conflicts and resolution workflow records.
- Attachment sessions and entity linkage.

### Core Tables (Suggested)

**Devices / Sessions**
- `devices(tenant_id, device_id) -> user_id, created_at, last_seen_at, app_version, status`
- `device_checkpoints(tenant_id, device_id) -> checkpoint_blob, updated_at`

**Operations (Append-Only)**
- `ops(tenant_id, ingest_seq, op_id, device_id, device_seq, entity_type, entity_id, base_version, patch, tombstone, scope_tags, schema_version, payload_hash, ingested_at)`
  - `ingest_seq` is server-assigned and strictly increasing (cursor basis).
  - Unique constraints:
    - `(tenant_id, device_id, device_seq)` for idempotent apply
    - `(tenant_id, op_id)` for stable operation identity
  - Index for pull:
    - `(tenant_id, ingest_seq)` (supports `WHERE ingest_seq > cursor ORDER BY ingest_seq LIMIT N`)

**Materialized Entity State**
- `entity_state(tenant_id, entity_type, entity_id) -> state_jsonb, version_summary_jsonb, deleted, updated_ingest_seq, updated_at`
  - `version_summary_jsonb` is a per-actor sequence summary used for causal/concurrent detection.

**Conflicts**
- `conflicts(tenant_id, conflict_id) -> entity_type, entity_id, detected_at, status, summary, payload_jsonb`

**Attachments**
- `attachment_sessions(tenant_id, attachment_id) -> sha256, size_bytes, content_type, status, uploader_device_id, entity_ref, created_at, committed_at`

### Partitioning & Retention
- Partition `ops` by time (e.g., monthly) to keep indexes manageable and retention easy.
- Retain ops for 30–90 days (tenant-configurable) while preserving longer-lived audit requirements via:
  - Separate immutable audit export (optional) or longer retention in `ops` for regulated tenants.

---

## Data Model

### Operation Model (Logical)
- `op_id`: `UUIDv7`
- `tenant_id`
- `actor_id`: `device_id` (recommended)
- `device_id`, `device_seq` (monotonic per device)
- `entity_type`, `entity_id`
- `base_version`: per-actor sequence summary (what the editor observed)
- `ingest_seq`: server-assigned ordering cursor (deterministic tie-breaker)
- `patch`: JSON Merge Patch / JSON Patch; typed sub-ops for counters/sets where used
- `tombstone`: delete marker
- `scope_tags`: authZ boundaries used for filtering
- `schema_version`
- `payload_hash`: detects “same op_id with different payload”

### Checkpoints (Opaque, Signed)
A checkpoint encodes:
- `tenant_id`
- `epoch`
- `cursor`: last applied/pulled `ingest_seq`
- optional protocol hints (compression, max batch sizes)
- `sig`: HMAC signature to prevent tampering and cross-tenant substitution

---

## Sync Flow

### Sequence
```mermaid
sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant C as Client
  participant E as Edge
  participant S as Sync API
  participant P as Postgres
  participant O as Object Store

  C->>E: POST /v1/sync/handshake
  E->>S: forward (auth context)
  S->>P: validate device + permissions
  S-->>C: checkpoint + limits

  C->>E: POST /v1/sync/push (ops batch)
  E->>S: forward
  S->>P: insert ops (idempotent)
  S->>P: merge into entity_state + conflicts
  S-->>C: acked_device_seq + new_checkpoint

  C->>E: GET /v1/sync/pull?checkpoint=...
  E->>S: forward
  S->>P: read ops since cursor (authZ filtered)
  S-->>C: ops[] + next_checkpoint + has_more

  C->>S: POST /v1/attachments/init
  S-->>C: pre-signed upload_url + attachment_id
  C->>O: PUT upload_url (resumable)
  C->>S: POST /v1/attachments/commit
  S->>O: HEAD/validate
  S-->>C: committed
```

### What Ensures Correctness
- **Idempotency**: `(tenant_id, device_id, device_seq)` uniquely identifies a causal “dot”.
- **Causality**: `base_version` is used to detect concurrency at the entity/field level.
- **Determinism**: merge rules use stable tie-breakers (`ingest_seq`, `op_id`) so replicas converge.
- **Server cursor**: checkpoints are server-issued and signed; clients never guess offsets.

---

## API Design

### Protocol
- REST over HTTPS for simplicity and debuggability.
- Optional upgrade path to gRPC for streaming/batching when needed.

### Endpoints
- `POST /v1/sync/handshake`
- `POST /v1/sync/push`
- `GET /v1/sync/pull?checkpoint=...&limit_ops=...&max_bytes=...`
- `POST /v1/attachments/init`
- `POST /v1/attachments/commit`

### Error Handling
Stable codes:
- `INVALID_ARGUMENT`, `UNAUTHENTICATED`, `PERMISSION_DENIED`, `RATE_LIMITED`
- `SCHEMA_INVALID`, `CHECKPOINT_EXPIRED`, `PAYLOAD_HASH_MISMATCH`, `ATTACHMENT_NOT_COMMITTED`

---

## Conflict Resolution

### Merge Strategy by Field Type
- **Commutative ops (lightweight CRDT-style)**
  - Counters: store and apply increments/decrements as additive ops.
  - Tag/checklist sets: apply add/remove ops with dot identifiers for idempotency.
- **Scalars**
  - LWW using `(ingest_seq, op_id)` tie-breaker for concurrent edits.
- **Workflows / Invariants**
  - State-machine transitions validated on apply; conflicting transitions produce a conflict record rather than overwriting.

### Conflict Records
When correctness cannot be guaranteed by merge rules:
- Persist a `conflicts` record with context for resolution.
- Return conflict summaries in `push` responses.
- Clients render “Needs review” and guide users through resolution.

---

## Scaling & Performance

### Performance Levers
- Batching: 200–1,000 ops/request with byte caps (e.g., 5 MB).
- Compression: zstd preferred; gzip fallback.
- Backpressure: `429` + `Retry-After` and server hints (`recommended_batch_ops`).
- Progressive catch-up: small initial pulls for user-visible freshness; then larger batches.

### Postgres Tactics (Practical)
- Bulk insert ops with `INSERT ... VALUES (...)` batching or `COPY`-style ingestion at the API layer.
- Partition `ops` by time; keep hot indexes small and cache-friendly.
- Use read replicas for pull-heavy traffic (handshake/pull) while keeping writes on primary.
- Keep merges transactionally consistent per operation batch to preserve RPO≈0 for acknowledged ops.

### Growth Path
- Start with one Postgres cluster (multi-AZ) + read replicas.
- If tenants outgrow a single cluster, introduce tenant-based database sharding behind the same Sync API contract.

---

## Failure Modes & Mitigations

1) **Client retries the same batch after timeout**
- Mitigation: unique `(tenant_id, device_id, device_seq)` and optional batch `Idempotency-Key` return consistent ack.

2) **Checkpoint expired (retention window exceeded or epoch change)**
- Mitigation: `CHECKPOINT_EXPIRED` triggers bounded resync:
  - server returns a scope-limited snapshot plus a new checkpoint
  - client preserves unacked local ops and replays them

3) **Conflicting edits to workflow-critical fields**
- Mitigation: semantic validation creates a conflict record and blocks finalization until resolved.

4) **Partial attachment upload**
- Mitigation: resumable upload; only allow entity references to `COMMITTED` attachments.

5) **Database degradation**
- Mitigation: shed load with `429`, reduce pull page sizes, prioritize handshakes and small pulls, and rely on multi-AZ durability for acked ops.

6) **AuthZ changes while offline**
- Mitigation: next handshake/pull enforces reduced scopes and can send best-effort redaction tombstones; optional hardening via per-scope encryption and key rotation.

---

## Operations

### Observability (SLO-Oriented)
Track:
- `push`/`pull` QPS, ops/s, bytes/s, error rate by code, P50/P95/P99 latency.
- Time-to-catch-up distribution and per-tenant backlog.
- Conflict rate by entity type/field and resolution time.
- Postgres health: replication lag, lock time, slow queries, partition bloat.
- Attachment init/commit success rate and checksum mismatch rate.

### Deployment & Compatibility
- Backward-compatible protocol evolution via handshake capabilities.
- Canary releases with dashboards on dedupe anomalies, conflict spikes, and checkpoint errors.
- Keep checkpoint decoding compatible for the last N epochs.

### Security & Compliance
- TLS everywhere; encryption at rest (KMS-managed).
- Minimize PII in logs; store structured audit trails with retention policies.
- Validate attachment size/type; malware scan via storage hooks/events; enforce tenant quotas.
- Signed checkpoints (HMAC) to prevent tampering and cross-tenant replay.

---

## Simplification Notes

- Removed: wide-column store; acceptable because the operation log + materialized state fit cleanly in partitioned Postgres with read replicas for pull traffic.
- Removed: Redis cache; acceptable because checkpoints and device metadata are small and served efficiently from Postgres with normal indexing and connection pooling.
- Removed: Kafka-derived stream in the core design; acceptable because analytics/search can be fed later from Postgres CDC without affecting sync correctness.
- Merged: gateway and separate sync service concerns into an Edge + single Sync API; acceptable because sync correctness benefits from a single transactional boundary.
- Complexity remaining: operation-based replication, signed checkpoints, and deterministic merges; necessary to support long offline periods, idempotent retries, and correct convergence with concurrent edits.
